{"id": "10c8b4a8-fc21-489b-9ea5-6f1cda751c8f", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:46:11.536107", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "011686a6-9a13-48f9-9097-875620d1697f", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The probe surfaced a real bug worth reporting: my peak-detection had an off-by-one (r[k] is ACF at lag k+1), which shifted every detected peak by −1 (983 vs 984 = 41×24, 335 vs 336 = 14×24). Fixing and re-running — this is exactly why the design needs a synthetic ground-truth pre-flight."}], "reasoning_content": "Excellent — this probe is highly informative and validates the researcher's insistence on specifying the extractor carefully. Findings (pure logic checks on synthetic data, in this sandbox):\n\nT1: pure sinusoid with period 24: the ACF peaks at 983, 959, 935, 887... spacing 24 apart! But 983 mod 24 = 983 - 40*24=983-960=23. Hmm, spacing is 24 but not multiples of 24. Why? Because the sum-based acf: sum over i of sin(2πi/24)*sin(2π(i+k)/24) — for pure noiseless sinusoid, the ACF is cos(2πk/24) modulated; peaks at k ≡ 0 mod 24. But 983 ≡ 23 mod 24. The discrepancy: with finite N and integer arithmetic drift, math.sin(2*math.pi*i/24) — i mod 24 cycles exactly but sin(2πi/24) for large i loses precision? No, more likely: because the series doesn't contain an integer number of periods? Actually 8000/24 = 333.33, not integer. The ACF for a truncated sinusoid: c(k) = (1/(N-k)) Σ_{i<N-k} s_i s_{i+k}. Σ s_i s_{i+k} = (N-k)/2 cos(ωk) + boundary oscillatory terms of O(1) amplitude, but after dividing by c0 ~ N/2... hmm those should be small. But the \"1/(n-k)\" bias-correction amplifies near kmax? No, 1/(n-k) at k=983: n-k=7017, factor fine.\n\nAlso c0 = Σ/N, and the mean is not exactly 0 (partial period) — mean subtraction handled. Hmm, the key thing: for k near 1000, cos(2πk/24): k=983 → 2π*23/24 → cos = 0.9659. Yes! 0.966 = cos(2π*1/24) = cos(15°) = 0.966. So all peaks at k ≡ ±1 mod 24 give cos = 0.966?? Wait cos(2π*23/24) = cos(-2π/24)=cos(15°)=0.966. Right. So r(k) ≈ cos(2π k/24) → maximum should be at k ≡ 0 mod 24 with value 1.0. But reported peaks have value 0.966, and there's presumably a higher value at 984 (=1.0) — but 984 isn't a local maximum because the ACF is flat-ish? No, cos(2πk/24) at k=984 would be 1.0 exactly, and neighbors 0.966 both sides, so 984 would be a strict local max with value 1.0 and would rank above 0.966. Unless my peak detection uses r[k]>=r[k-1] and r[k]>r[k+1] with float ties: if the ACF is exactly symmetric, r[k-1]==r[k+1]==0.966 and r[k]=1.0, so condition r[k]>=r[k-1] true, r[k]>r[k+1] true → 984 would be detected. Hmm but then sorted by -r, 1.0 would come first. Yet top-5 are all 0.966. So there is no peak at multiples of 24 with value 1.0? That suggests numerical precision: for large i (up to 8000), 2πi/24 large but sin computed fine.\n\nHmm, maybe the issue: my list comprehension `sum(d[i]*d[i+k] ...)` uses d = x - mean; mean of 333.33 periods of sine = nonzero ~ small. Fine.\n\nWait — the real question: are the values at k ≡ 0 mod 24 actually 1.0? The reported top peaks all 0.966, rounded to 3 decimals. Maybe the actual maxima are 0.9659 because of a phase/aliasing issue: the mean-subtracted truncated sinusoid... Let me not spend too long; but this is a genuinely useful observation for the design: naive local-maximum peak detection on a pure sinusoid's ACF fails / is numerically fragile, and my \"peak\" implementation had a bug: I looped `for k in range(2, len(r)-1)` where r[k] is ACF at lag k+1! Because r[0] corresponds to lag 1. That's an off-by-one! Indeed peaks reported as k but r[k-1] used in the print — I printed round(r[k-1],3) implying lag k ↔ r[k-1], but the peak detection compared r[k] values, i.e. lag k+1. So the detected \"peak lag\" is off by one → 984 (true max at lag 984=24·41) was missed, and 983 = 984-1 was reported as a peak because of the off-by-one indexing.\n\nSo the lesson: the off-by-one indexing bug in my own probe explains T1/T3/T4 anomalies (311 = 312-1, 335 = 336-1 = 14·24-1, 503 = 504-1 = 21·24-1, 550=551? hmm 550 vs multiples of 50: 550 = 11·50, ok that's exact; 48/96 ≈ 50 multiples minus 2? 48 isn't 50. And 48 vs 24: 48 = 2·24 exactly ✓).\n\nT2: 31 exceedances out of 500 lags at 95% CI — expected ~25; but the series was white noise so fine (this is consistent-ish; the point is CI works but is not per-lag exact).\n\nSo I should fix the off-by-one and re-run to get a clean logic check. This is a good demonstration of why the design must include \"extractor unit tests on synthetic series with known truth\" as a pre-flight verification step. Let me fix and rerun quickly.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cd /workspace/probe && python3 - <<'PY'\ns=open('acf_logic_check.py').read()\ns=s.replace(\"if r[k]>=r[k-1] and r[k]>r[k+1] and r[k]>ci:\",\"if r[k-1]>=r[k-2] and r[k-1]>r[k] and r[k-1]>ci:\")\ns=s.replace(\"            if abs(j/m-k)<=max(1,round(0.02*k)): alias=True\",\"            if abs(j/m-k)<=max(1,round(0.02*k)): alias=True\")\ns=s.replace('r=acf(noise(N,1.0),500)','r=acf(noise(N,1.0),500)')\ns=s.replace('print(\"T3 strong trend, RAW: naive argmax lag=\",r.index(max(r))+1,\"(true=24)\")','print(\"T3 strong trend, RAW: naive argmax lag=\",r.index(max(r))+1,\"(true=24)\")')\nopen('acf2.py','w').write(s)\nPY\ntimeout 600 python3 acf2.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-909bb5ec2dfb772b", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-909bb5ec2dfb772b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /workspace/probe && python3 - <<'PY'\\ns=open('acf_logic_check.py').read()\\ns=s.replace(\\\"if r[k]>=r[k-1] and r[k]>r[k+1] and r[k]>ci:\\\",\\\"if r[k-1]>=r[k-2] and r[k-1]>r[k] and r[k-1]>ci:\\\")\\ns=s.replace(\\\"            if abs(j/m-k)<=max(1,round(0.02*k)): alias=True\\\",\\\"            if abs(j/m-k)<=max(1,round(0.02*k)): alias=True\\\")\\n