that exact split sizes must be taken from the standard benchmark files, not invented.\n\nBetter to express cost in the itemized formula:\nfits = Σ_d Σ_h |G| × seeds, where |G| is grid size. Plus the L-grid only needs to be run once per (dataset, horizon) since CycleNet seeds 3. Plus baselines (seasonal naive: 0 fits; last value: 0; DLinear: 1 per (d,h) × 3 seeds), plus lookback-interaction subset (small: 4 arms × 4 datasets × 4 horizons × 3 seeds = 192). Let me construct numbers:\n\nDatasets: 4 real + 2 synthetic families × 3 realizations = 6 synthetic series → 10 series total (call S=10).\nHorizons H = 4.\nL grid |G| = designed to bracket: e.g. for ETTh1 candidate ~24: include 12,16,20,24,28,32,36,48,72,96,120,144,168,192,240,288,336,384,480,576,672,768,960 (log-spaced ~25 values) — I'll define grid construction rule: G = {L/2, L, 2L} ∪ D (domain defaults {24,168} ∪ freq-appropriate) ∪ log-spaced lattice over [2, L_max] with 25–30 points; total |G| ≈ 30 after de-dup.\nFits = 10 × 4 × 30 × 3 = 3,600. Plus baselines ~ (4 real + 6 synth) × 4 × 3 × 2 models = 240. Plus leakage arm: leakage only affects L selection, not fitting count — reuse fits where L coincides (leakage arm changes only the selected L → at most 1 extra L per (d,h) → +10×4×3 = 120). Plus rolling origins: 12 origins × (1 selected L + plateau bounds) — say 10 series × 12 origins × 3 seeds = 360 (only at H=336 to bound cost). Plus bootstrap stability: bootstrap is ACF-only (cheap, no fits) — B=200 resamples × ACF 2.5 s ≈ 500 s per series ×10 = 1.4 CPU-hours — worth itemizing! That's a real measured anchor: 200 × 2.5 s = 500 s ≈ 8.3 min per series single-core; parallel over 224 cores → trivial.\n\nTotal itemized ≈ 4,300 fits. Per-fit time τ unknown; express total = 4,300·τ; and note that CycleNet's parameter count is O(D·H + L·H) (a couple of thousand params), input windows 96–720, so a fit on ETTh1 train (~8.6k windows) is a small linear-model job; if τ ≤ 60 s → ≤ 72 CPU-hours single-core, ÷224 cores ⇒ sub-hour wall-clock at modest parallelism; if τ ≤ 5 s → ~6 CPU-hours. And name the reduced-scope fallback \"Fallback-R\": horizons {96, 720}, |G|=15, seeds 3, real datasets only for the grid (synthetic keep full) → fits = 4×2×15×3 + 6×4×30×3 = 360 + 2160 = 2520. Hmm synthetic full is the bigger part; also cut synthetic realizations to 2 → 4 series ×2×15×3=360 → total 720 + baseline. Good: name it precisely.\n\nNow, L grid must bracket candidates both sides: define grid range [2, L_max_grid] with L_max_grid = min(N_train/4, 4·L_candidate, 2000), and require the ACF candidate be interior: candidate ≥ g_min·2 and candidate ≤ g_max/2, else extend the grid (pre-registered bracketing rule). Good.\n\nPre-registered decision rules:\n- R1 (identifiability): accept that ACF identifies the period on a series if primary L_ACF is within ±5% or the nearest grid point to the calendar/truth period (real series: calendar period implied by frequency: ETTh1 hourly → 24 daily; ETTm1 15-min → 96 daily; Weather 10-min → 144 daily; Electricity hourly → 24 daily and 168 weekly).\nWait: ETTm1 15-min → daily = 96. Weather 10-min → 144. Electricity hourly → 24. ETTh1 → 24. Weekly for Electricity = 168. Good, these are the calendar references. And synthetic have known truth. So identifiability has an external ground truth for all 10 series — important strength.\n- R2 (optimality transfer): accept if regret ≤ 1 SE-of-grid-min AND L_ACF inside plateau, for ≥ 75% of (series × horizon) cells; primary metric MSE-normalized regret.\n- R3 (mechanism): L-matters-more-under-short-lookback interaction: expect |∂err/∂L| larger at lookback 96 than 720; if not, the ACF-L story is weaker.\n- R4 (leakage): report optimism gap = err_leakage_test − err_clean... actually leakage makes the test-set error *look* better only if test data influenced L selection; quantify gap in reported MSE between contaminated and clean selection, and gap in selected-L agreement with the test-period.\n- R5: if error-vs-L plateau covers > 50% of grid range → declare \"L is weakly identifiable\" and the answer becomes \"ACF narrows L to a wide basin; use ACF as an initializer/constraint, not as an exact selector.\"\n\nAnalysis plan: two-way (series × horizon) cell-level metrics; bootstrap CIs over seeds+blocks; Kendall's τ between ACF curve (negated) and error-vs-L curve on the grid — that's a nice direct measure of whether ACF *ranks* L correctly, better than argmin agreement. Add \"top-decile capture\": does ACF ranking put any plateau member in its top-k? Also a plot: err(L) and −ACF(L) on common axis for every series — the money figure.\n\nMultiple seasonality: CycleNet has single prototype → for dual-seasonal synthetic, the design asks: which choice of L (primary vs secondary vs neither) minimizes error, i.e. does CycleNet benefit more from matching the strong period or the short period? Report as the \"period-conflict\" experiment.\n\nAlso a \"cycle-length placebo\": L chosen by ACF on a phase-randomized (surrogate) version of the series → should land in the plateau no better than chance → establishes that the improvement isn't from arbitrary L values. And block-bootstrap/blocks: circular-block bootstrap on train window for L stability, B=200, block length = 2·L_candidate.\n\nLimitations: univariate target channel only; cross-channel period selection out of scope; CycleNet prototype assumes strict periodicity, calendar drift/holidays not modeled; real-series \"true period\" is a calendar convention not a ground truth; plateau may be flat; reimplementation fidelity; no numerical reproduction of published numbers claimed; CPU-only precludes transformer baselines at scale; 3 seeds limits dispersion estimates; ACF on train-only may differ from full-series ACF in nonstationary real data (Electricity has a distribution shift → L may change over time); grid quantization; synthetic realism gap; statistical power limited to ~40 cells.\n\nAlso the \"feasible checks\" — I already did the ACF logic checks and timings. I'll cite these in evidence, and note the two bugs found (off-by-one, harmonic de-dup polarity) which materially changed the pre-registered extractor spec.\n\nAlso: CycleNet fidelity section — prototype: the paper's CycleNet takes a learnable cyclic sequence of length L (the \"history prototype\" is derived from the average over cycles, actually the paper uses the average of the historical series aligned by phase — a fixed/cycle-averaged prototype X_c with period L), cyclic embedding: for input positions t_i, sample X_c[(t_i + offset) mod L] and add to the input; then a small MLP with residual connection; and loss on the normalized residual (y − prototype sample). Parameter count O(D·L_hidden) tiny. Fidelity checks: (a) param count matches the paper's order (≈ 10³–10⁴), (b) behavior: error must degrade when L is wrong (this is the expected L-sensitivity), (c) must beat DLinear at short lookback on ETTh1 (published qualitative behavior). Note that if the reimplementation fails these, the design's internal validity for claim (ii) is compromised → pre-registered \"go/no-go\" gate before running the main grid. That's a strong control (fidelity gate).\n\nNow write the result handoff. It should be complete but readable. Use headings. No fabricated numbers: any numbers I give about expected cost must be labeled as formulas/parameters, and the measured ones as sandbox measurements (ACF timings: 2.55s at N=36792 kmax=1000 etc.).\n\nLet me carefully separate: measured in sandbox (probe) vs design parameters.\n\nI'll write it now. Keep it organized and reasonably tight but complete.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-85e5492087a46642", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-85e5492087a46642", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# Experiment design: ACF-based identification of CycleNet's cycle length L\\n\\n## 0. Question, claims, unit of analysis\\nQuestion: can an autocorrelation analysis of the training history yield a CycleNet cycle length L that is as good as the best L obtainable by grid search?\\n\\n- **C1 (identifiability):** ACF/PACF peak extraction recovers the dominant period, with external ground truth available on all 10 series (known synthetic period; calendar period for real data).\\n- **C2 (optimality transfer):** the ACF-derived L falls inside the plateau of near-minimal forecast error and has low regret against the grid argmin.\\n- Unit of analysis: one **cell** = (series × forecast horizon). Primary sampling unit for CIs: block-bootstrap over the training window, plus 3 seeds.\\n\\n## 1. Series (fixed by your ruling)\\nReal, target channel only (univariate ACF): **ETTh1** (hourly → calendar day = 24), **ETTm1** (15-min → 96), **Weather** (10-min → 144), **Electricity** (hourly → 24 daily, 168 weekly).\\nSynthetic, known truth: family **S-A** single-seasonal, P ∈ {24, 50, 96, 168} (P=50 is deliberate: not a calendar divisor, so it tests whether the extractor finds a period with no domain prior); family **S-B** dual-seasonal (24, 168) — the 7:1 divisor case, an aliasing stress test — plus a coprime variant (50, 168). 3 noise seeds per configuration.\\nSplits: the standard benchmark train/val/test convention for each real file; synthetic use the same proportional split. **All ACF/PACF and all L selection use the train split only.** Exact split lengths are read from the data files, not assumed.\\n**Reference-truth definition (pre-registered):** real-series truth = the calendar period implied by the file's sampling frequency; this is a convention, not a ground truth, and is stated as a limitation.\\n\\n## 2. S1–S2: ACF input and computation\\nPreprocessing is a **first-class factor**, not a fixed detail (see probe finding F2 below): **T0** raw; **T1** mean-removed only; **T2** moving-average detrended (window 2·L̂+1, using a first-pass coarse estimate); **T3** first-differenced; **T4** log1p then T2 (non-negative series only: Electricity, Weather subsets).\\nEstimator: bias-corrected autocovariance (divide by N−k), normalized to r(0)=1. Max lag **L_max = min(N_train/4, 1000)**. Significance band: naive ±1.96/√N **and** Bartlett-adjusted bands, both stored; peaks must clear the band. PACF (Yule–Walker / Durbin–Levinson, computed by hand-coded recursion if statsmodels is unavailable) is stored as a confirmatory view, not a selector, and PACF-at-lag-L vs ACF-at-lag-L agreement is reported descriptively.\\n\\n## 3. S3: candidate extraction (pre-registered, informed by the sandbox probes)\\n**Primary extractor `ACF-F` (fundamental-peak):**\\n1. find local maxima of r(k) over a ±2 lag window, k ≥ 3, requiring r(k) > CI and r(k) > 0;\\n2. keep the \\\"near-maximal\\\" set: peaks with r(k) ≥ (1−ε)·max peak, **ε = 0.05** fixed a priori;\\n3. **fundamental selection:** among near-maximal peaks, take the smallest k whose integer multiples (tolerance 2 %) cover ≥ 70 % of the near-maximal set;\\n4. secondary period = next near-maximal peak that is not an integer multiple of the primary (reported, never fed to the model);\\n5. no peak clears the band → **declared uninformative**, fall back to the calendar default L (pre-registered rule R5 below).\\nReported alternates (the naive-extractor comparison you asked for): `E1` raw argmax ACF; `E2` smallest-lag-above-0.9·max without a local-max requirement; `E3` FFT periodogram argmax (zero-padded, Hann window, parabolic interpolation); `E4` differenced-series ACF argmax (T3); `E5` `ACF-F` with ε ∈ {0.02, 0.10} to expose hyperparameter sensitivity; `E6` Hipel–McCombs-style multi-peak search.\\n**Two polarity/hygiene rules fixed by the probes:** peaks are indexed against the r(1)-based array (an off-by-one silently shifted every candidate by −1 — F1); harmonic de-duplication must **keep the fundamental and discard multiples**, not the reverse (F3).\\n\\n## 4. S4: oracle L grid (bracketing, per your ruling 7)\\n`G = {2L_ACF, L_ACF, L_ACF/2} ∪ {domain defaults for the series frequency: 24, 96, 144, 168, 336, 672} ∪ a log-spaced lattice of ~25–30 points spanning [2, min(N_train/4, 4·L_ACF_upper, 2000)]`, de-duplicated (≈30 points). **Bracketing rule (pre-registered):** the grid is extended until the ACF candidate and every near-maximal candidate lie strictly inside the grid with at least two lattice points on each side; a regret number is reported only for cells where this holds, otherwise flagged \\\"edge-artefact, excluded\\\". The full error-vs-L curve is a primary deliverable per cell, not a supplement.\\n\\n## 5. S5: models, arms, fidelity gate\\n**Reimplementation (fidelity section, no numerical reproduction of published figures claimed):** CycleNet = fixed cycle-averaged prototype of length L indexed by phase; cyclic embedding `X_c[(t + Δ) mod L]` added to the input; cyclic component subtracted before the head; shared feed-forward head with residual connection; standardization by train-window statistics. Fidelity gates, run **before** the main grid (go/no-go): (a) parameter count in the published 10³–10⁴ order, not 10⁶; (b) error must degrade when L is set far from the calendar period (expected L-sensitivity); (c) at short lookback it must beat DLinear on at least one ETT split, the published qualitative behaviour. If (a)–(c) fail, C2 is not testable in this environment and that is the finding, reported as such.\\n**Arms per cell:** A1 `L = L_ACF`; A2 oracle `argmin_L` on the **validation** split (never test) — the regret reference; A3 fixed domain default; A4 `L_ACF/2`; A5 `2·L_ACF`; A6 L from each alternate extractor `E1–E6` (identification comparison); A7 phase-randomized surrogate ACF-derived L (**placebo**); A8 random L from the grid (chance reference); A9 `L = 1` (prototype effectively disabled, ablation).\\n**Lookback-interaction sub-study (mechanism test):** {lookback 96, lookback 720} × {A1, A2, A9}. Prediction: L-sensitivity is much larger at lookback 96; if the curve is flat at long lookback, the practical value of ACF-derived L is conditional on short-input settings — this bounds the scope of any claim.\\n**Baselines:** seasonal naive at `L_ACF`, seasonal naive at calendar period, last value, DLinear. PatchTST is **excluded** under CPU-only feasibility and named as a scope limitation rather than promised.\\n**Leakage contrast (control i):** arm **A10** selects L using ACF computed on train+val+test (full series) — the standard contamination — and arm **A11** uses ACF on train+test. Report the **optimism gap** (A10 test MSE − A1 clean test MSE) and the **selection divergence** (does contamination move L across the plateau boundary?), i.e. quantify how much the reported advantage of \\\"tuned L\\\" is artefactual.\\n\\n## 6. S6: metrics and pre-registered decision rules\\nPer cell: MSE/MAE on the standardized test split, mean and dispersion over 3 seeds.\\n- **Plateau (pre-registered, your ruling 4):** `P = {L in G : err(L) ≤ min_G err + SE(min_G err)}`, SE from the 3 seeds.\\n- **Regret:** `err(L_ACF) − min_G err`, both absolute and relative to `min_G err`.\\n- **Plateau hit rate:** fraction of cells with `L_ACF ∈ P` — the primary success metric.\\n- **Rank agreement:** Kendall's τ between ACF value and forecast error over G per cell (does ACF *rank* L correctly, not just pick it); plus **top-decile capture** (does the ACF's top-k candidate list contain any member of P).\\n- **Exact-argmin hit rate:** retained as a secondary, honestly-reported metric.\\n- **Stability:** circular-block bootstrap of the train window, B = 200, block length 2·L̂ → modal L and 95 % interval per series; **rolling origins:** 12 origins at H = 336, L re-selected from origin-available data only → L drift over time (expected on Electricity, which drifts).\\n- **Decision rules:** **R1** identifiability holds for a cell if L_ACF is within 5 % of the reference period; **R2** C2 is supported if plateau hit rate ≥ 75 % *and* median relative regret ≤ 5 % across cells; **R3** mechanism supported if |∂err/∂L| at lookback 96 exceeds that at 720; **R4** leakage contamination is material if the optimism gap exceeds one seed SE; **R5** if the plateau spans > 50 % of the grid range, the cell is declared **weakly identifiable** and the conclusion is stated as \\\"ACF locates L within a wide basin; use it as a constraint/initializer, not an exact selector\\\" — this is a finding, not a failure. Headline figure: `err(L)` and `−ACF(L)` on a shared L axis for every series, plateau shaded.\\n\\n## 7. Feasibility and itemised cost (no budget supplied; none invented)\\nFits = `S × H × |G| × seeds` = 10 × 4 × ~30 × 3 ≈ **3,600**, plus ~240 baselines, ~120 leakage-arm selections, ~360 rolling-origin fits, ~300 lookback-sub-study fits → **≈ 4,600 fits**. Cost = 4,600 × τ, where τ = per-fit wall time, **not measured** (no torch/numpy here). Extrapolation argument: CycleNet is a near-linear model with ~10³–10⁴ parameters over ≤ 8.6k training windows, so τ is expected to be seconds-to-minute scale on one CPU core; at τ = 5 s ≈ 6.4 CPU-hours, at τ = 60 s ≈ 77 CPU-hours, divided by the available 224-way affinity → sub-hour to a few hours of wall clock at modest parallelism. **Named reduced-scope fallback `Fallback-R`** if τ lands at the high end: horizons {96, 720}; |G| = 15 (coarser lattice, plateau quantization reported explicitly); real series keep the full grid, synthetic reduced to 2 noise seeds → ≈ 1,100 fits (≈ 4× reduction), with no change to any pre-registered rule.\\nACF-side cost **is measured** in this sandbox (pure Python, no numpy): r(k) to k = 1000 takes 2.55 s at N = 36,792, 2.39 s at N = 34,461, 1.81 s at N = 26,304, 0.57 s at N = 8,645. Hence the full ACF factor grid (10 series × 5 preprocessings × 3 extractors) is a few CPU-seconds, and the 200-resample bootstrap is ~8 min per series single-core — negligible either way, including under Fallback-R.\\n\\n## 8. Limitations\\n(1) Target-channel ACF only; cross-channel/multivariate period selection is explicitly out of scope. (2) Real-series \\\"truth\\\" is a calendar convention, so R1 is weaker evidence than on synthetic data. (3) Real data are non-stationary (Electricity drift), so a single train-window L may be an average over regimes; rolling origins quantify but cannot remove this. (4) CycleNet has one prototype, so dual seasonality is characterized, not modeled; the (24,168) divisor case is intrinsically ambiguous to any ACF-based rule, and the coprime (50,168) case is included to separate genuine model limits from aliasing artefacts. (5) Reimplementation, not the official code — C2 is a statement about CycleNet's *architecture class*, not a reproduction of published numbers. (6) 3 seeds gives crude SEs and the SE-based plateau may be optimistically narrow. (7) Grid quantization makes plateau edges a function of |G|. (8) No transformer baseline. (9) Synthetic realism gap: no holidays, missing values, or level shifts. (10) The design is unexecuted: everything above is a protocol plus sandbox feasibility evidence, and no forecast-error number is claimed anywhere.\\n\\n## 9. What the sandbox probes already established (feasibility, not results)\\nZero-dependency execution of the identification logic is confirmed, and four findings materially changed the protocol: **F1** an off-by-one in peak indexing shifted every candidate by −1 (983 vs 984 = 41×24; 335 vs 336 = 14×24) — now a fixed indexing rule plus a synthetic pre-flight test; **F2** raw ACF with trend returns **argmax at lag 1** instead of the true period, so preprocessing must be a factor, not a constant; **F3** naive argmax on near-sinusoidal series returns an arbitrary high multiple (P=24 → 168; P=50 → 100; dual 24+168 with L_max=800 → 672), and naive \\\"smallest lag above 0.9·max\\\" returns 2 or 23 because AR persistence inflates short lags — hence the local-maximum + multiple-coverage rule in `ACF-F`; **F4** with correct de-duplication `ACF-F` recovers P = 24/50/96/168 and 168-primary on (24,168), while **failing under trend on raw input and at low SNR** (returning 5×24 or 10×50) — which fixes the identifiability stress factors (SNR, trend) that the synthetic families must span, and motivates rule R5's explicit \\\"ACF is uninformative → calendar default\\\" branch rather than pretending the extractor always works. Naive ±1.96/√N false positives measured at 16.6 per 300 lags vs a nominal 15.0, confirming the band is only approximately calibrated and justifying reporting Bartlett bands alongside.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository/environment inspection (all direct observations):\\n- /workspace contains only task.json (412 B); cat shows question/context and documents.track.md as a STRING (\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") — no track.md file on disk.\\n- find / -iname \\\"*cyclenet*\\\": no matches → no CycleNet code. ls /opt /srv /data /datasets: absent; find / -maxdepth 4 -name \\\"*.csv\\\" → only OS vendor files → no benchmark series.\\n- python3 = 3.14.4; numpy, scipy, pandas, statsmodels, sklearn, torch all ModuleNotFoundError; pip 25.1.1 present but urllib to pypi.org → URLError \\\"Temporary failure in name resolution\\\" → no installs. Only setuptools/pip wheels in /usr/share/python-wheels. No GPU (per brief).\\n\\nSandbox feasibility probes written and run this turn (pure stdlib, /workspace/probe/):\\n- acf2.py: peak detection measured against analytic expectations — pure sinusoid P=24 peaks at lags 888/912/936/960/984 (all multiples of 24, ACF≈1.0) after the off-by-one fix; pre-fix output was 983/959/935 (shifted −1) → finding F1.\\n- acf3.py: \\\"P=24 +trend RAW: naive argmax lag=1 (true=24)\\\"; \\\"single P=24 naive_argmax=168\\\", \\\"single P=50 naive_argmax=100\\\"; dual 24+168 with L_max=800/1200 → naive returns 672 (4×168); \\\"fundamental@0.90\\\" without a local-max rule returned 2 or 23 → findings F2, F3.\\n- acf3.py calibration: 20 white-noise series × 300 lags, mean exceedances beyond ±1.96/√N = 16.6 vs nominal 15.0.\\n- acf4.py (local-max + 2% multiple-coverage + ε=0.05 near-max set): primary recovered exactly for P=24, 50, 96, 168, for the trended case only after MA detrending (raw → no peak clears the band), and 168 primary for dual (24,168); failures at low SNR (120 = 5×24 raw, 240 detrended) and on dual (50,168) → 500 = 10×50 → fixes the SNR/trend stress factors and rule R5.\\n- cost_probe.py measured pure-Python ACF to kmax=1000: 2.55 s @ N=36792, 2.39 s @ N=34461, 1.81 s @ N=26304, 0.57 s @ N=8645 → ACF and bootstrap stages are cheap even with no BLAS; per-model-fit time τ remains unmeasured and is explicitly parameterized in the cost estimate.\\n\\nStated as knowledge, not as repo observation: CycleNet models long periodicity via a length-L cycle prototype sampled at phase t mod L with an additive cyclic embedding and a residual feed-forward head, with parameter count in the 10^3–10^4 range and a short lookback; therefore L is a first-order hyperparameter and CPU training is plausible. The reimplementation's parameter-count/behaviour fidelity gates exist precisely because no local code was available to check against.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Real benchmark files will be supplied or obtained on a networked machine at execution time; none exist in this sandbox and none were invented.\", \"'Reference period' for real series means the calendar period implied by sampling frequency (ETTh1 24, ETTm1 96, Weather 144, Electricity 24 daily / 168 weekly); the true optimal L need not equal the calendar period and any divergence is a reported result, not an error.\", \"Standard per-dataset train/val/test conventions are used and split lengths are read from the files rather than assumed; target channel only (OT / Wet-Bulb style) for the ACF.\", \"Standard splits and per-dataset preprocessing follow the common benchmark convention; the exact number of channels is irrelevant because the ACF is univariate on the target.\", \"The oracle L grid is scored on the validation split; test data is never used for L selection except in the deliberately contaminated leakage arms A10/A11.\", \"'Near-minimal' is defined by the 1-SE plateau with SE from exactly 3 seeds; more seeds would narrow the plateau and could lower plateau hit rates.\", \"Per-fit wall time τ is unmeasured (no torch/numpy available); all cost figures are multiples of τ, and the CPU-hour statements are extrapolations from CycleNet's ~10^3–10^4 parameter architecture, not measurements.\", \"3 seeds with dispersion reported, per the ruling; block bootstrap (B=200, block 2·L) is treated as the stability device because seeds alone cannot capture sampling variability of the ACF.\", \"Reimplementation stands in for the official repository, so conclusions are about the CycleNet architecture class rather than a specific published implementation.\", \"No experiment has been executed: the probe runs are dependency-free logic and timing checks on synthetic data, and no forecast error value is reported anywhere in this design.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"τ (per-fit wall time) is unknown; the 4,600-fit design is feasible for τ up to roughly a minute at 224-way CPU affinity, beyond which Fallback-R (~1,100 fits) must be triggered.\", \"Whether error-vs-L curves are sharp or flat on the four real series. The pre-registered plateau rule plus rule R5 make the design informative either way, but the strength of claim C2 depends on it.\", \"Whether the CycleNet reimplementation passes the three fidelity gates; if it does not, C2 cannot be tested here and the design yields a negative feasibility finding instead.\", \"Whether the fixed ε=0.05 near-max threshold and 70 % multiple-coverage rule transfer from synthetic series to real data; E5 varies ε as a sensitivity arm, but no tuning on test data is permitted to rescue it.\", \"Whether 3 noise seeds per synthetic configuration is enough to map the SNR identifiability boundary that probe F4 exposed.\", \"Whether the low-SNR extractor failure mode also occurs on Weather/Electricity target channels; if it does, rule R5 (calendar default fallback) will fire often and weaken the headline conclusion.\", \"Rolling-origin L drift on Electricity could exceed the plateau width, in which case a static L is the wrong target and that becomes the paper's main message.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-11022000-7344-4700-9047-eb1985f32222", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
