{"id": "e32c11c6-708d-4cfa-bda4-5afaaae999bc", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:54:49.505738", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "6fc69785-4145-4db6-b2b0-63f717448bcd", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Stage field got dropped (probably due to size/format). Resubmit with stage explicitly first.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment design: ACF-based identification of CycleNet's cycle length L\n\n## 0. Question, claims, unit of analysis\nQuestion: can an autocorrelation analysis of the training history yield a CycleNet cycle length L as good as the best L obtainable by grid search?\n\n- **C1 (identifiability):** ACF/PACF peak extraction recovers the dominant period, with external ground truth on all 10 series (known synthetic period; calendar period for real data).\n- **C2 (optimality transfer):** the ACF-derived L falls inside the plateau of near-minimal forecast error with low regret against the grid argmin.\n- Unit of analysis: one **cell** = (series × horizon). CIs from circular-block bootstrap over the train window plus 3 seeds.\n\n## 1. Series (fixed by your ruling)\nReal, target channel only: **ETTh1** (hourly → 24), **ETTm1** (15-min → 96), **Weather** (10-min → 144), **Electricity** (hourly → 24 daily, 168 weekly).\nSynthetic with known truth: **S-A** single-seasonal P ∈ {24, 50, 96, 168} (50 is deliberate: no calendar prior, so it tests whether the extractor can find a period with no domain default); **S-B** dual-seasonal (24,168) — a 7:1 divisor, i.e. an aliasing stress case — plus coprime (50,168). 3 noise seeds per configuration.\nStandard train/val/test conventions per dataset; split lengths read from the files, not assumed. **All ACF/PACF and all L selection use train only.** Reference truth (pre-registered): real = calendar period implied by sampling frequency; a convention, listed as a limitation.\n\n## 2. S1–S2: ACF input and computation\nPreprocessing is a first-class factor (probe finding F2): **T0** raw; **T1** mean-removed; **T2** MA-detrended (window 2·L̂+1 from a coarse first pass); **T3** first-differenced; **T4** log1p then T2 (non-negative series only).\nBias-corrected autocovariance (divide by N−k), normalized to r(0)=1; **L_max = min(N_train/4, 1000)**. Bands: naive ±1.96/√N **and** Bartlett, both stored; peaks must clear the band. PACF (hand-coded Durbin–Levinson if statsmodels unavailable) is a confirmatory view, never a selector; ACF-vs-PACF agreement at the selected lag is reported descriptively.\n\n## 3. S3: candidate extraction (pre-registered, probe-informed)\n**Primary `ACF-F` (fundamental-peak):** (1) local maxima of r(k) over a ±2-lag window, k ≥ 3, with r(k) > CI and > 0; (2) near-maximal set: r(k) ≥ (1−ε)·max peak, **ε = 0.05** fixed a priori; (3) **fundamental selection**: smallest near-maximal k whose integer multiples (2 % tolerance) cover ≥ 70 % of the near-maximal set; (4) secondary = next near-maximal peak that is not a multiple of the primary — reported, never fed to the model; (5) no peak clears the band → declared **uninformative**, calendar-default L (rule R5).\nAlternates (naive-extractor comparison): `E1` raw argmax; `E2` smallest lag above 0.9·max with no local-max rule; `E3` FFT periodogram argmax (zero-padded, Hann, parabolic interpolation); `E4` differenced-series ACF argmax; `E5` `ACF-F` at ε ∈ {0.02, 0.10}; `E6` Hipel–McCombs-style multi-peak search.\nTwo hygiene rules fixed by the probes: peak indexing is against the r(1)-based array (an off-by-one silently shifted every candidate −1, F1); harmonic de-duplication keeps the **fundamental** and discards multiples, never the reverse (F3).\n\n## 4. S4: oracle L grid (bracketing per ruling 7)\n`G = {2L_ACF, L_ACF, L_ACF/2} ∪ {frequency-appropriate defaults 24, 96, 144, 168, 336, 672} ∪ log-spaced lattice of ~25–30 points over [2, min(N_train/4, 4·L_ACF_upper, 2000)]`, de-duplicated (≈30 points). **Bracketing rule:** extend the grid until the ACF candidate and every near-maximal candidate are strictly interior with ≥2 lattice points on each side; regret is reported only for cells satisfying this, others flagged \"edge artefact, excluded\". The full error-vs-L curve is a primary deliverable per cell.\n\n## 5. S5: models, arms, fidelity gate\n**Reimplementation fidelity (no numerical reproduction of published figures claimed):** fixed cycle-averaged prototype of length L indexed by phase; cyclic embedding `X_c[(t+Δ) mod L]` added to the input; cyclic component subtracted before the head; shared feed-forward head with residual; train-window standardization. Gates run **before** the main grid (go/no-go): (a) parameter count in the published 10³–10⁴ order; (b) error degrades when L is set far from the calendar period; (c) beats DLinear at short lookback on ≥1 ETT split. Failure of (a)–(c) means C2 is untestable here, reported as such.\n**Arms per cell:** A1 `L_ACF`; A2 validation-split oracle argmin (regret reference); A3 fixed domain default; A4 `L_ACF/2`; A5 `2L_ACF`; A6 L from each alternate extractor E1–E6; A7 phase-randomized-surrogate ACF L (**placebo**); A8 random grid L (chance); A9 `L=1` (prototype ablated).\n**Lookback-interaction sub-study (mechanism test):** lookback {96, 720} × {A1, A2, A9}; prediction: |∂err/∂L| much larger at 96. If the curve is flat at long lookback, the value of ACF-derived L is conditional on short-input settings, which bounds the claim.\n**Baselines:** seasonal naive at `L_ACF`, seasonal naive at calendar period, last value, DLinear. PatchTST **excluded** on CPU-only grounds and named as a scope limitation rather than promised.\n**Leakage contrast (control i):** A10 selects L from ACF on train+val+test (full series); A11 from train+test. Report the **optimism gap** (A10 − A1 test MSE) and the **selection divergence** (does contamination move L across the plateau boundary?), quantifying how much of any \"tuned-L advantage\" is artefactual.\n\n## 6. S6: metrics and pre-registered decision rules\nPer cell: MSE/MAE on standardized test data, mean + dispersion over 3 seeds.\n- **Plateau:** `P = {L : err(L) ≤ min_G err + SE(min_G err)}` (SE from the 3 seeds).\n- **Regret:** `err(L_ACF) − min_G err`, absolute and relative.\n- **Plateau hit rate** (primary success metric); **Kendall's τ** between ACF value and error across G per cell (does ACF *rank* L correctly); **top-decile capture** (does the ACF's top-k list contain any plateau member); **exact-argmin hit rate** kept as a secondary, honestly-reported metric.\n- **Stability:** circular-block bootstrap B=200, block 2·L̂ → modal L + 95 % interval; **rolling origins** 12 origins at H=336 with origin-available data only → L drift (expected on Electricity).\n- **Rules:** **R1** identifiability if L_ACF within 5 % of reference period; **R2** C2 supported if plateau hit rate ≥ 75 % *and* median relative regret ≤ 5 %; **R3** mechanism supported if |∂err/∂L|(96) > |∂err/∂L|(720); **R4** leakage material if optimism gap > 1 seed SE; **R5** if the plateau spans > 50 % of the grid range the cell is **weakly identifiable** and the conclusion becomes \"ACF locates L within a wide basin; use it as a constraint/initializer, not an exact selector\" — a finding, not a failure. Headline figure: err(L) and −ACF(L) on a shared L axis per series, plateau shaded.\n\n## 7. Feasibility and itemised cost (no budget supplied; none invented)\nFits = `S×H×|G|×seeds` = 10×4×~30×3 ≈ **3,600**, + ~240 baselines + ~120 leakage selections + ~360 rolling-origin + ~300 lookback sub-study ≈ **4,600 fits**, cost = 4,600·τ where τ = per-fit wall time, **not measured** (no torch/numpy). Extrapolation: CycleNet has ~10³–10⁴ parameters over ≤8.6k windows, so τ should be seconds-to-a-minute per fit on one core: τ=5 s ≈ 6.4 CPU-hours, τ=60 s ≈ 77 CPU-hours; over 224-way affinity → sub-hour to a few hours wall clock. **Fallback-R** if τ lands high: horizons {96,720}, |G|=15 (plateau quantization reported explicitly), full grid on real series, synthetic reduced to 2 seeds → ≈1,100 fits (≈4×), with no change to any pre-registered rule.\nACF cost **is measured** here in pure Python: r(k) to k=1000 takes 2.55 s @ N=36,792, 2.39 s @ 34,461, 1.81 s @ 26,304, 0.57 s @ 8,645 → the ACF factor grid (10 series × 5 preprocessings × extractors) is a few CPU-seconds and the B=200 bootstrap ≈8 min per series single-core, negligible under either scope.\n\n## 8. Limitations\n(1) Target-channel ACF only; cross-channel period selection out of scope. (2) Real \"truth\" is a calendar convention, so R1 is weaker than on synthetic data. (3) Non-stationarity (Electricity drift) means a single train-window L averages regimes; rolling origins quantify but cannot remove this. (4) One prototype ⇒ dual seasonality is characterized, not modeled; (24,168) is intrinsically ambiguous to any ACF rule, and the coprime (50,168) case separates genuine model limits from aliasing artefacts. (5) Reimplementation, not official code: C2 speaks to the architecture class, not a published implementation. (6) 3 seeds give crude SEs and may make the SE-plateau optimistically narrow. (7) Grid quantization makes plateau edges a function of |G|. (8) No transformer baseline. (9) Synthetic realism gap: no holidays, missing values, level shifts. (10) The design is unexecuted; no forecast-error number is claimed anywhere.\n\n## 9. What the sandbox probes already established (feasibility, not results)\nZero-dependency execution of the identification logic is confirmed, and four findings changed the protocol. **F1** an off-by-one in peak indexing shifted every candidate by −1 (983 vs 984 = 41×24; 335 vs 336 = 14×24) → fixed indexing rule + synthetic pre-flight test. **F2** raw ACF of a trended series returns **argmax at lag 1** instead of the true period → preprocessing must be a factor, not a constant. **F3** naive argmax on near-sinusoidal series returns an arbitrary high multiple (P=24→168, P=50→100, dual 24+168 at L_max=800→672), while naive \"smallest lag above 0.9·max\" returns 2 or 23 because AR persistence inflates short lags → hence the local-maximum + multiple-coverage construction of `ACF-F`. **F4** with corrected de-duplication `ACF-F` recovers P = 24/50/96/168 and 168-primary on (24,168), but **fails under trend on raw input and at low SNR** (returning 5×24 or 10×50) → fixes the SNR/trend stress factors the synthetic families must span and motivates R5's explicit \"uninformative → calendar default\" branch instead of assuming the extractor always works. Naive ±1.96/√N false positives measured at 16.6 per 300 lags vs nominal 15.0, so Bartlett bands are reported alongside.", "evidence": "Repository/environment inspection (direct observations):\n- /workspace contains only task.json (412 B); cat shows documents.track.md as a STRING (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\") — no track.md file on disk.\n- find / -iname \"*cyclenet*\": no matches → no CycleNet code. ls /opt /srv /data /datasets: absent. find / -maxdepth 4 -name \"*.csv\" → only OS vendor files → no benchmark series present.\n- python3 3.14.4; numpy/scipy/pandas/statsmodels/sklearn/torch all ModuleNotFoundError; pip 25.1.1 present but urllib to pypi.org → URLError \"Temporary failure in name resolution\" → no installs possible. Only setuptools/pip wheels in /usr/share/python-wheels. No GPU.\n\nSandbox probes written and run this turn (pure stdlib, /workspace/probe/):\n- acf2.py: after the off-by-one fix, a pure sinusoid P=24 shows ACF peaks at lags 888/912/936/960/984 (all multiples of 24, r≈1.0); pre-fix output was 983/959/935 → finding F1.\n- acf3.py: \"P=24 +trend RAW: naive argmax lag=1 (true=24)\"; \"single P=24 naive_argmax=168\"; \"single P=50 naive_argmax=100\"; dual 24+168 at L_max=800/1200 → naive = 672 (4×168); smallest-lag-above-0.9·max returned 2 or 23 → findings F2, F3.\n- acf3.py calibration: 20 white-noise series × 300 lags → mean exceedances beyond ±1.96/√N = 16.6 vs nominal 15.0.\n- acf4.py (local max + 2 % multiple coverage + ε=0.05): primary exact for P=24, 50, 96, 168; trended case exact only after MA detrending (raw → no peak clears the band); 168 primary for dual (24,168); low SNR → 120 (5×24) raw and 240 detrended; dual (50,168) → 500 (10×50) → fixes the SNR/trend stress factors and rule R5.\n- cost_probe.py measured pure-Python ACF to kmax=1000: 2.55 s @ N=36792, 2.39 s @ 34461, 1.81 s @ 26304, 0.57 s @ 8645 → ACF/bootstrap stages cheap with no BLAS; per-model-fit time τ remains unmeasured and is parameterized in the cost estimate.\n\nStated as knowledge, not as a repo observation: CycleNet models long periodicity via a length-L cycle prototype sampled at phase t mod L with an additive cyclic embedding and a residual feed-forward head, parameter count in the 10^3–10^4 range, short lookback; hence L is a first-order hyperparameter and CPU training is plausible. The reimplementation fidelity gates exist precisely because no local code was available to check against.", "assumptions": ["Real benchmark files will be supplied or obtained on a networked machine at execution time; none exist in this sandbox and none were invented.", "'Reference period' for real series = calendar period implied by sampling frequency (ETTh1 24, ETTm1 96, Weather 144, Electricity 24/168); the optimal L need not equal it and divergence is a reported result, not an error.", "Standard per-dataset splits are used with lengths read from the files; ACF is univariate on the target channel only (univariate/multivariate channel-selection is out of scope, not silently assumed away).", "The oracle L grid is scored on validation data; test data informs L selection only in the deliberately contaminated leakage arms A10/A11.", "'Near-minimal' is defined by the 1-SE plateau with SE from exactly 3 seeds; more seeds would narrow the plateau and could reduce hit rates.", "Per-fit wall time τ is unmeasured (no torch/numpy); all cost figures are multiples of τ, extrapolated from CycleNet's ~10^3–10^4 parameter architecture, not measured.", "3 seeds with dispersion reported per the ruling; block bootstrap B=200 (block 2·L) carries the stability analysis because seeds alone cannot capture ACF sampling variability.", "A faithful reimplementation stands in for the official repository, so conclusions concern the CycleNet architecture class.", "No experiment has been executed: the probe runs are dependency-free logic/timing checks on synthetic data, and no forecast-error value is reported anywhere in this design."], "uncertainties": ["τ (per-fit wall time) is unknown; the ~4,600-fit design is feasible up to roughly a minute per fit at 224-way affinity, above which Fallback-R (~1,100 fits) must be triggered.", "Whether error-vs-L curves are sharp or flat on the four real series; the plateau rule and R5 keep the design informative either way, but the strength of C2 depends on it.", "Whether the CycleNet reimplementation passes the three fidelity gates; if not, C2 is untestable here and the outcome is a negative feasibility finding.", "Whether ε=0.05 and the 70 % multiple-coverage rule transfer from synthetic to real series; E5 varies ε as a sensitivity arm, but no test-set tuning is permitted to rescue them.", "Whether 3 noise seeds per synthetic configuration suffices to map the SNR identifiability boundary that probe F4 exposed.", "Whether the low-SNR extractor failure also occurs on Weather/Electricity target channels; frequent R5 firing would weaken the headline conclusion.", "Whether rolling-origin L drift on Electricity exceeds the plateau width, which would make a static L the wrong target and become the main finding."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8b79b6cdbf649632", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-8b79b6cdbf649632", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment design: ACF-based identification of CycleNet's cycle lengt